  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A student answers a multiple-choice test. Each question of the test has four possible answers, and only one choice is correct. The student knows the correct answer for $60%$ of the questions. If the student decides to select an answer at random for the questions that she does not know the answer to, what is the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that the selected question is correct, and let $K$ be the event that the student knew the answer. Then ${K}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the student did not know the answer.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected question is correct, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{\u2032})P({K}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the student knows the answer to $60%$ of the questions, we have\n\n$$\nP(K)=0.6,\\,P({K}^{\u2032})=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since each question has four possible answers, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|K)=1,\\,P(C|{K}^{\u2032})=\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected question on the test is correct is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|K)P(K)+P(C|{K}^{\u2032})P({K}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =(1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.6+0.1 \\\\  & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
